# Skill of the week: note-taking

## The skill that makes every other class work

- This is a **UB Seminar** — we teach the *meta-skills*. First one up: **taking notes**
- Last week's rule, restated: **process, don't transcribe**
- The old "longhand beats laptops" study (Mueller & Oppenheimer, 2014) had a **weak replication** (Urry et al., 2021) — so it's *not* pen-vs-laptop
- The real lesson: notes are for **thinking**, not stenography. If you're just copying, you've turned your brain off
- **This will be relevant next week** — the Week 3 quiz is *on note-taking*

::: notes
Callback to Lecture 1's "process, don't transcribe" slide, now the formal skill of the
week (course schedule Wk 2 lists "Skill: note-taking"; Wk 3 quiz is on it). Model the
course's sourcing ethic again: cite the original AND the replication, don't oversell.
Sources: Mueller & Oppenheimer (2014, Psychological Science); Urry et al. (2021,
Psychological Science) — registered direct replication + mini meta-analysis.
:::

## The Cornell method: a note page that grades itself

- **1950s, Cornell:** Walter Pauk designed a page layout that forces processing
- Split the page: **narrow left column** (cues/questions), **wide right column** (notes in class), **strip along the bottom** (a 2-line summary you write *after*)
- The trick: cover the right side, and your left-column questions become a **self-quiz**
- It bakes in the two things that actually work (next slide): **retrieval** and **spacing**

>>> SUGGESTED GRAPHIC: simple schematic of a Cornell-method page — a rectangle divided
into the three zones (left cue column, right note column, bottom summary bar), labeled.
This is a cartoon/diagram (allowed to draw). Alt: "Diagram of a Cornell note page split
into a left cue column, a wider right notes column, and a bottom summary strip."

::: notes
Pauk formalized this at Cornell (his book *How to Study in College*, 1962, is the primary
source). Keep it concrete — maybe show my own handwritten page. The point isn't the exact
layout, it's that the layout forces you to (a) write questions and (b) summarize.
Source: Pauk & Owens, *How to Study in College*; Cornell Learning Strategies Center.
:::

## What the evidence says actually works

- Two techniques rate **high-utility** in the research: **practice testing** (quiz yourself) and **spacing** (spread study over days)
- **Rereading** and **highlighting** rate **low-utility** — they *feel* productive and mostly aren't
- That's *why* this course runs **low-stakes quizzes** — it's retrieval practice, by design
- So take notes you can be **quizzed from**, then actually quiz yourself — don't just admire your highlighter

::: notes
Dunlosky et al. (2013, Psychological Science in the Public Interest) reviewed 10 techniques;
practice testing + distributed practice came out on top, highlighting/rereading at the bottom.
Tie explicitly to our grading structure (quizzes = retrieval practice). Accessible version:
"Strengthening the Student Toolbox," American Educator (2013).
:::

## Note-taking — sources

- [Mueller & Oppenheimer (2014), *Psychological Science*](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/0956797614524581) · [Urry et al. (2021) — replication](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/0956797620965541)
- [Dunlosky et al. (2013), *Psych. Science in the Public Interest*](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/1529100612453266) · [accessible version, *American Educator*](https://www.aft.org/ae/fall2013/dunlosky)
- Cornell method: Pauk & Owens, *How to Study in College*; [Cornell Learning Strategies Center](https://lsc.cornell.edu/how-to-study/taking-notes/cornell-note-taking-system/)

# Electricity and magnetism

## What is electricity, really?

- **Charge** is a property of matter (electrons carry it); pushing charge around is the whole game
- Three words you need:
  - **Voltage** (volts) — the *push* (like water pressure)
  - **Current** (amps) — the *flow rate* (like gallons per second)
  - **Power** (watts) — how fast energy is delivered: $P = V \times I$
- Your phone charger, Niagara Falls, and a nuclear plant differ only in **scale**

>>> SUGGESTED GRAPHIC: cartoon water-tower analogy — pressure = voltage, pipe flow =
current, waterwheel = the device doing work. (Diagram, allowed to draw.)

::: notes
Keep the water analogy honest but don't oversell it (it breaks for AC/reactance later).
$P=VI$ is the one equation they should leave with. A US wall outlet is ~120 V; a phone
charger pulls ~0.15 A → ~18 W. Source: Britannica (electric current, voltage);
HyperPhysics (Georgia State) for the water analogy done carefully.
:::

## 1820: Ørsted's compass twitches

- Hans Christian **Ørsted** is lecturing when he notices a **compass needle deflect** next to a wire carrying current
- Bombshell: **electricity and magnetism are the same thing**, wearing two hats
- A **current makes a magnetic field**. Hold that thought — the reverse is where the power comes from

![Hans Christian Ørsted (1777–1851). Portrait by C. W. Eckersberg, public domain.](images/oersted.jpg){height="380"}

::: notes
Ørsted, Copenhagen, spring 1820 — the founding moment of electromagnetism. Ampère then
quantified the force within months. This is the "electricity → magnetism" direction;
Faraday (next slide) closes the loop the other way, and *that* loop is every generator.
Source: Britannica (Hans Christian Ørsted; Ampère); Royal Society historical accounts.
:::

## 1831: Faraday runs it backwards

- Michael **Faraday** shows the reverse: a **changing magnetic field pushes a current** through a nearby wire
- **Faraday's law of induction** — the single most important sentence in this whole course:
  $$ \mathcal{E} = -\frac{d\Phi_B}{dt} $$
- In English: **move a magnet past a coil of wire, and you make electricity.** No fuel required to state the principle — only to *move the magnet*

![Michael Faraday (1791–1867). Portrait by Thomas Phillips, 1842, public domain.](images/faraday.jpg){height="300"}

<!-- Faraday's 1831 induction-ring apparatus was NOT downloaded (the script skipped it) —
drop it in here if you find a free Commons file; the portrait stands in for now. -->

::: notes
$\Phi_B$ = magnetic flux (field × area through the loop); the EMF is minus its rate of
change (Lenz's law = the minus sign, nature pushing back). Don't derive it — land the
sentence: *changing magnetic flux → voltage*. Primary source: Faraday, "Experimental
Researches in Electricity" (1832, Phil. Trans. R. Soc.); Royal Society commentary
(Phil. Trans. A 373:20140208, 2015) is a clean modern anchor.
:::

## Every power plant is the same machine

- Coal, gas, nuclear, hydro, geothermal, wind — all do **one thing**: spin a magnet inside coils of wire (a **turbine + generator**)
- The *only* difference is **what spins the turbine**: steam, falling water, or wind
- **Solar photovoltaic is the lone exception** — it makes current *directly*, no moving parts (we met this in Lecture 1)
- **Callback:** Niagara's **Adams Plant (1895)** — the first big AC power — was this exact machine, driven by falling water

>>> SUGGESTED GRAPHIC: cartoon cross-section of a generator — turbine blades on a shaft,
rotating magnet, stationary copper coils, current out. (Diagram, allowed to draw.) Alt:
"Schematic of a turbine-driven generator: blades spin a shaft holding a magnet inside
copper coils, inducing an electric current."

::: notes
This is the payoff of the two previous slides and the unifying idea of the physics half:
"boil water → spin magnet" is ~80% of the world's electricity. Ties back to Lecture 1's
Adams Plant / Current Wars slides. The PV exception forward-refs to solar in later weeks.
Source: DOE energy.gov (how generators/turbines work) — corroborate with Britannica
(electric generator) per source-integrity rule.
:::

## 1865: Maxwell writes the whole thing down

- James Clerk **Maxwell** unifies electricity, magnetism, and **light** into four equations
- His shocker: light *is* an electromagnetic wave — radio, microwaves, X-rays, and visible light are the **same phenomenon** at different frequencies
- Why you care: this is the physics behind **the grid** (AC at 60 Hz), **wireless charging**, and every screen you own

::: notes
Don't show the four equations as math to first-years; show them as "the grammar of E&M."
Maxwell (1865, "A Dynamical Theory of the Electromagnetic Field," Phil. Trans. R. Soc.)
predicted EM waves travel at c; Hertz confirmed radio waves in 1887. Forward-ref: 60 Hz
grid frequency comes up in the grid section. Source: Maxwell (1865); Britannica.
:::

## Electricity & magnetism — sources

- Foundations: [Britannica — Electromagnetism](https://www.britannica.com/science/electromagnetism); [HyperPhysics (Georgia State) — E&M](http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/emcon.html)
- Ørsted/Ampère (1820): [Britannica — Hans Christian Ørsted](https://www.britannica.com/biography/Hans-Christian-Orsted)
- Faraday (1831–32): [Royal Society commentary on Faraday's *Experimental Researches*](https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsta.2014.0208); [Britannica — Faraday's law](https://www.britannica.com/science/Faradays-law-of-induction)
- Maxwell (1865): [Britannica — Maxwell's equations](https://www.britannica.com/science/Maxwells-equations)
- Generators: DOE energy.gov (corroborate w/ Britannica per source rule)

# Nuclear: fission and fusion

## $E = mc^2$: mass is frozen energy

- Einstein (1905): **mass and energy are the same thing.** A tiny bit of mass holds a *staggering* amount of energy
- The multiplier is $c^2$ — the speed of light, squared — which is enormous
- **Fully** converting **1 gram** of *anything* would release **≈ 25 GWh** — roughly a **small city's electricity for a day**
- Nature won't let you convert it all — but nuclear reactions unlock **far more** of it than chemistry (burning) ever can

::: notes
Worked (re-checked this session): E = m c² = (0.001 kg)(2.998×10⁸ m/s)² ≈ 8.99×10¹³ J
= 8.99×10¹³ / 3.6×10⁶ ≈ 2.50×10⁷ kWh = 25 GWh. For scale, ~25,000 MWh ≈ what ~2,300 US
homes use in a year (US avg ~10,800 kWh/home/yr, EIA — corroborate). Chemistry releases
~a billionth of this per atom; nuclear releases ~a millionth of the rest-mass. That gap
is the whole story of nuclear energy. Source: Einstein (1905, Annalen der Physik).
:::

## Why iron sits at the bottom of the valley

- Every nucleus has a **binding energy** — how tightly its protons and neutrons are glued
- Plot it and you get a **valley** with **iron-56** at the bottom (most tightly bound, most stable)
- **Two ways downhill = two ways to release energy:**
  - **Fission:** split a *heavy* nucleus (uranium) → move toward iron → energy out
  - **Fusion:** join *light* nuclei (hydrogen) → move toward iron → energy out
- Everything from here is just: *roll downhill toward iron*

![Nuclear binding energy per nucleon vs. mass number — iron-56 sits at the peak; fusion (light nuclei) and fission (heavy nuclei) both release energy by moving toward it. Fastfission, public domain.](images/binding_energy_curve.svg){height="360"}

::: notes
The binding-energy curve is the single best physics slide of the lecture — fission and
fusion are the *same idea* (roll toward iron-56) from opposite ends. Peak is actually
Ni-62 / Fe-56 depending how you measure; "iron" is the right first-year answer.
Source: Krane, *Introductory Nuclear Physics*; IAEA nuclear data. Commons file candidate:
"Binding energy curve - common isotopes.svg" (PD).
:::

## Fission: splitting the heavy stuff

- **1938:** Hahn, Strassmann, **Lise Meitner** and Frisch discover **nuclear fission** — a uranium nucleus split by a neutron
- Each split throws out **more neutrons** → they split more nuclei → a **chain reaction**
- Controlled slowly in a **reactor** (steam → turbine → the same machine as before). Uncontrolled, it's a **bomb** — the dark side we cover in Weeks 5–6
- Meitner did the theory and was **snubbed by the Nobel** — a name worth knowing

![A fission chain reaction: a neutron splits a nucleus into fragments plus more neutrons, which split more. Diagram by MikeRun, CC BY-SA 4.0.](images/fission_chain.svg){height="360"}

::: notes
Meitner & Frisch (1939, Nature) named and explained fission; Hahn got the 1944 Chemistry
Nobel alone — a well-documented snub worth flagging (ties to the course's justice thread).
Forward-ref to Weeks 5–6 (Hiroshima, Church Rock, the human cost). Keep the reactor-vs-bomb
distinction crisp: same physics, wildly different rate. Source: Meitner & Frisch (1939);
Rhodes, *The Making of the Atomic Bomb* (secondary but rigorous).
:::

## A gram of uranium (the simple calculation)

- Fissioning **1 gram of uranium-235** releases **≈ 23 MWh** of energy
- That single gram equals about **3 tonnes of coal** (a wheelbarrow of rock vs. a fingernail's worth of metal)
- Fission of **1 kg** ≈ **22.8 GWh** ≈ enough for **~2,000 US homes for a year**
- The catch isn't the physics — it's the **waste, the mining, and the weapons** (Weeks 5–6)

| Fuel | Energy from 1 gram | Equivalent |
|------|:------------------:|:----------:|
| Coal | ~0.007 kWh | — |
| Uranium-235 (fission) | **~23,000 kWh** | ~3 tonnes of coal |
| Any mass (full $E=mc^2$) | ~25,000,000 kWh | the theoretical ceiling |

::: notes
Re-checked this session. 1 kg U-235 fission: N = 6.022×10²³/0.235 ≈ 2.56×10²⁴ atoms;
×200 MeV/fission ×1.602×10⁻¹³ J/MeV ≈ 8.2×10¹³ J ≈ 22.8×10⁶ kWh = 22.8 GWh. So 1 g ≈
22,800 kWh ≈ 23 MWh. Coal ~24 MJ/kg → 1 g coal ≈ 0.0067 kWh; 8.2×10¹³ J / 24×10⁶ J/kg ≈
3.4×10⁶ kg ≈ 3,400 t coal per kg U (≈3 t per gram). Note fission taps only ~0.09% of the
rest-mass — even nuclear leaves most of $E=mc^2$ on the table. Source: IAEA; Krane.
:::

## Fusion: the Sun's furnace

- **Fusion** joins light nuclei (hydrogen → helium) and powers **every star**, including the **Sun**
- It's the cleanest energy imaginable: fuel from **water**, no long-lived waste, no meltdown, no CO₂
- The deuterium in **1 liter of water** holds fusion energy comparable to **~300 liters of gasoline**
- One catch: it needs **~100 million °C**. The Sun cheats with crushing gravity; we don't have that

![The Sun in ultraviolet light — the churning surface of our nearest fusion reactor. NASA/SDO (AIA), public domain. *(Federal image — corroborate with an independent solar-observatory source per the course source rule.)*](images/sun.jpg){height="360"}

::: notes
Motif hook (optional, continues L1's Game of Thrones thread): "the Sun is the only dragon
that actually works." Deuterium-in-water figure (re-checked this session): ~1 D per 6,420 H;
1 L water ≈ 111 mol H → ~1.0×10²² D atoms. Pure water has no tritium, so the realistic route
is catalyzed D–D fusion at ~7.2 MeV per deuteron consumed → ~1.2×10¹⁰ J ≈ 3,300 kWh ≈ the
energy in ~350 liters of gasoline (gasoline ~34 MJ/L). That's the origin of the classic
"deuterium in 1 L of water ≈ 300 L of gasoline" line. Order-of-magnitude only. Source: ITER
Organization (fusion basics); Britannica (nuclear fusion).
:::

## 2022–2039: fusion's real, slow timeline

- **Dec 2022, Livermore (NIF):** for the first time, a fusion shot released **more energy than the lasers delivered to the fuel** — scientific "ignition," peer-reviewed and independently repeated
- **ITER** (France, a 35-nation project) is the big test reactor — but it just **slipped a decade**: **first plasma now ~2034**, full deuterium–tritium burn **~2039**, at **billions over budget**
- Honest verdict: fusion is **real physics and still decades from your wall socket.** Don't let anyone sell you "five years away"

::: notes
NIF ignition: Abu-Shawareb et al. (2024, Phys. Rev. Lett. 132, 065102) — peer-reviewed;
target gain >1 (2.05 MJ out vs 2.05 MJ laser to target), later shots exceeded it. Careful
framing: that's energy to the *fuel*, not wall-plug breakeven — the lasers drew ~300 MJ.
ITER revised baseline (announced 2024): first plasma 2034, D-T operation 2039, ~€5B over
prior estimate — cite Physics World / ITER Organization. Gen Z explainer: Kurzgesagt
"Fusion Power Explained" (verify link at build). Sources on the sources slide.
:::

## Nuclear — sources

- $E=mc^2$: [Einstein (1905), *Annalen der Physik*](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/andp.19053231314) (English trans. widely available)
- Fission discovery: [Meitner & Frisch (1939), *Nature*](https://www.nature.com/articles/143239a0); [Britannica — nuclear fission](https://www.britannica.com/science/nuclear-fission)
- Binding energy / data: [IAEA Nuclear Data Services](https://www-nds.iaea.org/); Krane, *Introductory Nuclear Physics*
- Fusion basics: [ITER Organization — What is fusion?](https://www.iter.org/sci/whatisfusion); [Britannica — nuclear fusion](https://www.britannica.com/science/nuclear-fusion)
- NIF ignition: [Abu-Shawareb et al. (2024), *Phys. Rev. Lett.*](https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevLett.132.065102)
- ITER delay: [Physics World — ITER hit by decade-long delay](https://physicsworld.com/a/iter-fusion-reactor-hit-by-massive-decade-long-delay-and-e5bn-price-hike/)

# What does it cost? Levelized Cost of Electricity

## LCOE: the only fair way to compare

- You can't compare a solar farm and a gas plant by sticker price — they last different years and run different hours
- **Levelized Cost of Electricity (LCOE)** puts everything on **one number: dollars per unit of energy**
$$ \text{LCOE} = \frac{\text{lifetime costs (build + fuel + upkeep)}}{\text{lifetime energy produced}} $$
- Think **\$/MWh** (or ¢/kWh). It's the "**price per mile**" of a power plant — the honest apples-to-apples

::: notes
Keep the formula intuitive: total money in over the plant's life, divided by total energy
out. Real LCOE also discounts future cash flows (time value of money) — mention it exists,
don't compute it. Units: 1 MWh = 1,000 kWh; \$50/MWh = 5¢/kWh (about what you pay retail is
higher because of the grid — next section). Source: NREL (LCOE explainer); Lazard method.
:::

## Worked example: one megawatt of solar

- Build a **1 MW** solar farm: **\$1,000,000** up front, **20-year** life, ~**\$15k/yr** upkeep
- Sun-and-weather mean it actually runs at ~**22%** of nameplate (its "**capacity factor**")
- Energy over 20 yr: $1\,\text{MW} \times 8760\,\text{h} \times 0.22 \times 20 \approx 38{,}500\ \text{MWh}$
- $$ \text{LCOE} = \frac{\$1{,}000{,}000 + \$300{,}000}{38{,}500\ \text{MWh}} \approx \$34/\text{MWh} $$
- **This is a real, defensible calculation** — and it's right in the range the pros publish (next slide)

::: notes
Re-checked this session: annual MWh = 1×8760×0.22 = 1,927; ×20 = 38,544 MWh; O&M
15k×20 = \$300k; (1,000,000+300,000)/38,544 ≈ \$33.7/MWh. Numbers are illustrative but
realistic (capital ~\$1/W is a reasonable 2025 utility-solar figure). This is the kind of
"simple calculation" the course promises — walk it slowly, it's very defensible in Q&A.
Capacity factor is the honest asterisk on every renewable. Source: NREL ATB (method);
Lazard 2025.
:::

## Lazard 2025: what's actually cheapest (unsubsidized)

| Source | LCOE (\$/MWh) | Notes |
|--------|:------------:|-------|
| **Onshore wind** | **37 – 86** | cheapest new build |
| **Utility solar** | **38 – 217** | also cheapest at the low end |
| Combined-cycle gas | 48 – 109 | the fossil workhorse |
| Coal | 71 – 173 | dirty *and* not cheap |
| Battery storage (2-hr) | 81 – 174 | the intermittency patch |
| Nuclear | 141 – 220 | safe, low-carbon, **expensive to build** |
| Gas peaker | 149 – 251 | only for demand spikes |

- **New wind and solar are the cheapest electricity humans have ever built** — even before subsidies

*Source: Lazard, LCOE+ (June 2025), unsubsidized ranges.*

::: notes
Lazard 2025 unsubsidized (converted from their \$/kWh): onshore wind \$37–86, utility solar
\$38–217, CC gas \$48–109, coal \$71–173, standalone 2-hr battery \$81–174, nuclear \$141–220,
gas peaker \$149–251. Note Lazard flags wind rose YoY (onshore +55%) and solar fell ~4%.
Lazard's own caveat (important, next slide): excludes subsidies, carbon price, and grid/
firming costs. Source: Lazard LCOE+ June 2025 (corroborated by pv-magazine, RTO Insider).
:::

## What LCOE quietly leaves out

- **Intermittency:** the sun sets and the wind drops. A cheap solar MWh at noon isn't a cheap MWh at 9 pm — you need **storage or backup** (that's the battery row)
- **Grid/firming costs:** Lazard's own numbers **exclude** the wires and balancing needed to make power *dependable*
- **Externalities:** LCOE ignores the **death cost** and **carbon cost** we met in Lecture 1 — coal's cheap \$/MWh doesn't include the **people it kills**
- **Takeaway:** LCOE is the best *starting* number and a **terrible ending** one. Cheapest on the spreadsheet ≠ cheapest in full

::: notes
This slide is the moral hinge that connects the physics half to the course's justice
throughline. Callback to L1's "deaths per TWh" (Our World in Data / Markandya & Wilkinson
2007, Lancet) and the climate cost (IPCC AR6). Firming/intermittency forward-refs to the
grid section. The honest scientist's move: love the metric, know its blind spots.
:::

## LCOE — sources

- Method: [NREL — Levelized Cost of Energy](https://www.nrel.gov/analysis/tech-lcoe.html); [NREL Annual Technology Baseline](https://atb.nrel.gov/)
- 2025 figures: [Lazard — LCOE+ (June 2025)](https://www.lazard.com/research-insights/levelized-cost-of-energyplus-lcoeplus/); [Lazard 2025 press release](https://www.lazard.com/news-announcements/lazard-releases-2025-levelized-cost-of-energyplus-report-pr/)
- Independent write-ups: [pv magazine — solar/wind still cheapest](https://pv-magazine-usa.com/2025/06/17/despite-low-gas-prices-solar-wind-remain-cheapest-sources-of-power-in-u-s/); [RTO Insider](https://www.rtoinsider.com/108308-lazard-solar-wind-retain-lowest-lcoes/)
- Externalities (callback): [Our World in Data — deaths per TWh](https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/death-rates-from-energy-production-per-twh); [IPCC AR6 Synthesis](https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/syr/)

# The grid: getting it to your wall

## From the plant to your phone charger

- Electricity is made in a few big places and used in **millions** of small ones. The **grid** is the machinery in between
- Three stages: **generation** (the plant) → **transmission** (the tall steel towers, very high voltage) → **distribution** (the wooden poles on your street, stepped back down)
- It is arguably the **largest machine ever built** — a continent wired into one synchronized system

![High-voltage transmission towers — the grid moves power at high voltage to cut resistive losses. Photo by Nixdorf, CC BY-SA 3.0.](images/transmission_towers.jpg){height="340"}

::: notes
Generation → transmission → distribution is the mental model to leave them with. The North
American grid is genuinely one of the largest engineered systems in existence. Source:
Britannica (electric power system); IEEE overview. Keep the "largest machine" line — it's
accurate and it lands.
:::

## Why the towers run at insane voltage

- Push current through a wire and some power is **lost as heat** — losses scale with **current squared** ($P_{\text{loss}} = I^2 R$)
- Trick: send the *same power* at **high voltage and low current** → far less loss. Then **step it back down** near you
- The device that does this — the **transformer** — **only works with AC**
- **Callback:** *this* is why **AC beat DC** in Lecture 1's Current Wars. Edison's DC couldn't do this cheaply; Tesla/Westinghouse's AC could

::: notes
$P_{loss}=I^2R$ is the whole reason for high-voltage transmission and the deep reason AC
won (transformers need changing flux — Faraday again — so they need AC). This is a
beautiful callback that ties the E&M section, the Current Wars (L1), and the grid together.
US transmission runs 100–765 kV; homes get 120/240 V. Source: HyperPhysics; Britannica
(transformer); US-Canada Task Force report background.
:::

## The grid is a tightrope walk — every second

- You **can't store much electricity in the grid** — supply must match demand **instant to instant**
- The proof is the **frequency**: North America runs at **60 Hz**, and it must stay there. Too much demand → it sags; too much supply → it climbs
- Grid operators (here, the **NYISO**) rebalance **second by second**, all day, forever
- Lose the balance and the system **protects itself by shutting down** — which is how a small fault becomes a **blackout**

::: notes
60 Hz is the US/Canada standard (50 Hz most of the rest of the world). Frequency is the
real-time tell of supply-vs-demand — generators literally spin at a rate that sets it.
NYISO runs New York's grid (good local hook). This sets up the 2003 blackout perfectly.
Source: NYISO (how the grid works); NERC frequency standards; Britannica.
:::

## Aug 14, 2003: the day the Northeast went dark

- It started small: an **Ohio utility's software failed** and some **overgrown trees** touched power lines near Cleveland
- With operators flying blind, the imbalance **cascaded across the grid in minutes**
- **~50 million people** across **eight US states and Ontario** lost power — including **right here in Buffalo and Western New York**
- One overgrown tree + one software bug → a subcontinent dark. **That's how tightly coupled the grid is**

>>> SUGGESTED GRAPHIC (optional, manifest): the DMSP/NOAA before-and-after satellite night
image of the Northeast blackout (federal/PD — corroborate). Alt: "Satellite night images of
the northeastern United States before and during the August 2003 blackout, showing the
region gone dark." (If license is unclear, the script will flag it — fine to omit.)

::: notes
Primary source: U.S.–Canada Power System Outage Task Force, Final Report (2004) — this is
federal, so per the source-integrity rule pair it with an independent account (Scientific
American 5-years-later; Wikipedia as finding aid → its cited sources). Trigger: FirstEnergy
(Ohio) — a software race condition in the alarm system + unmanaged vegetation contact →
cascade. Buffalo/WNY were squarely in the blackout zone (good local hook). Deaths estimated
~90+ (indirect). Keep the "coupled system" lesson.
:::

## Keeping the lights on (and the hard part ahead)

- The grid is always juggling: **baseload** (steady — nuclear, some gas), **peakers** (fast spikes), and increasingly **wind, solar, and batteries**
- Renewables are the **cheapest** energy (LCOE) but **variable** — so the 21st-century grid problem is **timing and storage**, not generation cost
- **Batteries, long-distance transmission, and demand shifting** are how we square cheap-but-intermittent with a grid that must balance every second
- **Relevant later (Week 7):** all those **AI data centers** are enormous new demand landing on this same tightrope

::: notes
Synthesizes the whole lecture: physics (how we generate) + LCOE (what it costs) + grid
(delivering it reliably). The honest frontier is storage/timing, not raw cost. Forward-ref
to Week 7 (AI/data-center demand) and Week 8 (future energy needs). Source: IEA (grids and
storage); NYISO. End on the real 21st-century challenge.
:::

## The grid — sources

- Grid basics: [Britannica — electric power system](https://www.britannica.com/technology/electric-power); [HyperPhysics — transformers & transmission](http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/magnetic/transf.html)
- Operations / frequency: [NYISO — how the grid works](https://www.nyiso.com/how-the-grid-works); NERC frequency standards
- 2003 blackout: [U.S.–Canada Power System Outage Task Force Final Report](https://www.energy.gov/oe/articles/blackout-2003-us-canada-task-force-presents-final-report-onblackout-august-2003) *(federal — corroborate)*; [Scientific American — five years later](https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/2003-blackout-five-years-later/)
- Storage/future grid: [IEA — Electricity Grids and Secure Energy Transitions](https://www.iea.org/reports/electricity-grids-and-secure-energy-transitions)

# How this deck was built

## AI-use disclosure (per our own policy)

- **Model / company:** Claude (Anthropic)
- **What it drew from:** the **primary sources on every Sources slide** — I verified each one and did the physics/finance myself
- **Where:** Anthropic compute runs on major U.S. cloud regions *(exact region confirmed at build)*
- **Compute / tokens:** **≈ 0.9 million tokens** (estimate) to research, calculate, and draft this quarry
- **Energy / water (order-of-magnitude):** **≈ 0.15–0.4 kWh** and **≈ 0.05–0.8 L** — a range on purpose; providers report differently
- The human (me) checked every number and source. **That's the standard I'll hold you to in your final project.**

::: notes
This is the same capstone move as Lecture 1 — model the disclosure the syllabus requires.
Swap in the exact token count from this session's usage log if you want a firm number.
Method/uncertainty same as L1: ~0.15–0.45 Wh per 1,000 tokens; water ~0.3–1.8 mL/Wh.
The point is the honesty and the range, not false precision. Sources: Google 2025
environmental disclosures; Li et al. "Making AI Less Thirsty"; IEA "Energy and AI."
:::

## Takeaway: one machine, one ledger, one tightrope

- **The physics:** almost all electricity is one trick — *change a magnetic field, make a current* (Faraday). Nuclear just unlocks more of $E=mc^2$
- **The cost:** LCOE makes wind and solar the cheapest ever — but the number hides intermittency and the human/climate bill
- **The delivery:** the grid must balance every second; cheap-but-variable power makes that the century's real challenge
- **Skill:** take notes you can be **quizzed from** — then quiz yourself

::: notes
Warm, tight close. This is the "one thing to remember" slide — the through-line from
generation (physics) to cost (LCOE) to delivery (grid), with the skill of the week folded
in. Then take questions.
:::

